  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through Monday)\nIssued at 132 PM CDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nTemperatures decrease a bit more on Thursday as the ridge remains \nwest of our forecast area. Highs top out in the mid-to-upper 90s for \nmost, with 100 degree temperatures confined mainly to the river \nvalleys. Remnant outflow from previous overnight convection and \nupslope flow allows low (generally 10-30%) rain chances to stick \naround for portions of the Permian Basin, Lower Trans-Pecos, and in \nthe higher terrain Thursday afternoon. Additional showers and storms \nmay move into our southeast New Mexico counties that evening from \nthe west. Over the weekend, the ridge starts to shift back towards \nwest Texas and southeast New Mexico. However, by this time the ridge \nwill have become relatively weaker and more broad thanks to passing \nshortwaves to the north. So, while temperatures remain above normal \nby late August standards, they also will not be as hot as the past \nfew days. Highs generally top out in the upper 90s and right around \n100 degrees for most locations through early next week. After \nThursday, besides some lingering rain chances in and around the \nhigher terrain Friday (10-30%), most locations will stay dry with \nthe ridge moving back this way over the weekend.\n\nSprang",
